cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use swiftsites, please cite it as below, and also cite snp-sites, from which it derives."
title: "swiftsites: a byte-identical drop-in replacement for snp-sites"
type: software
version: 1.0.0
date-released: 2026-11-04
license: GPL-3.0-or-later
repository-code: "https://github.com/joe-b-christensen/swiftsites"
authors:
  - family-names: Christensen
    given-names: Joe
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0007-0908-1299"
    email: joechristensen@clin.au.dk
    affiliation: "Translational Neuropsychiatry Unit (TNU), Department of Clinical Medicine, Aarhus University, Denmark"
keywords:
  - snp-sites
  - SNP
  - multiple sequence alignment
  - bacterial genomics
  - phylogenetics
  - VCF
  - performance
abstract: >-
  swiftsites reduces a whole-genome multiple sequence alignment to its variable columns with output byte-identical to
  snp-sites 2.5.1 for every mode (multi-FASTA, VCF, PHYLIP, pseudo-reference, pure-ACGT, monomorphic and constant-site
  output; plain, gzipped and FASTQ input). On four real benchmark sets it was 43x faster than snp-sites with two threads
  and 26x with one, and on seven real inputs 5.0x faster than SNPick writing variable-site FASTA (geometric means, cloud
  VM with 2 vCPUs).
references:
  - type: article
    title: "SNP-sites: rapid efficient extraction of SNPs from multi-FASTA alignments"
    authors:
      - family-names: Page
        given-names: Andrew J.
      - family-names: Taylor
        given-names: Ben
      - family-names: Delaney
        given-names: Aidan J.
      - family-names: Soares
        given-names: Jorge
      - family-names: Seemann
        given-names: Torsten
      - family-names: Keane
        given-names: Jacqueline A.
      - family-names: Harris
        given-names: Simon R.
    journal: "Microbial Genomics"
    volume: 2
    issue: "4"
    year: 2016
    doi: "10.1099/mgen.0.000056"
